00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
this morning we're sharing the  remarkable story of a brave young boy  whose diagnosis for a mysterious  condition  is expected to help solve future medical  puzzles researchers recently discovered  a genetic disorder that causes  four-year-old lucario  to produce no antibodies it led to an  immune system so weak that he was  constantly sick  senior medical correspondent dr tara  narulis spoke with luke's family about  their years-long search for answers  tara good morning good morning vlad this  is a condition that had never been seen  before in any medical literature it was  truly a medical mystery  luke was experiencing infection after  infection  and no treatment was working that's when  doctors at the children's hospital of  philadelphia stepped in  with a diagnosis that helped change the  course of his life  today four and a half year old luke  theriot looks  like a normal healthy boy  oh there he is there's my handsome guy  but just a few years ago luke was sick  almost every day  and his health was deteriorating around  seven eight months old he was just tired  he looked different  his eyes were watery and kind of  red underneath he had a really runny  nose  he started to get some red spots on his  face something was different and i  couldn't really explain it  for months he was in and out of the  hospital with no diagnosis  until a test revealed he had no  antibodies and was unable to build a  healthy immune system  if you don't have antibodies it usually  means that you don't have the cells  called b cells that are the factories  for antibodies and as a result you  get infections lots of infections so are  these  two nutrients yeah at the children's  hospital of philadelphia  dr neil romberg and his team of  immunologists ran  every test possible without finding the  cause of luke's sickness  eventually they mapped his entire  genetic sequence  and discovered he had a mutation so rare  it had no name  and had only been seen in mice he had a  mutation in a gene called spy one that  he did not inherit from his mother and  that he did not inherit from his father  this is what we call a de novo mutation  those are extremely suspicious so this  de novo or new mutation  as far as you know had never been seen  anywhere before  it was novel had never been seen before  you ready  spy 1 explained why luke couldn't make b  cells  but it came with another issue there was  a concern  that luke was going to be predisposed to  cancer  a lot of human leukemias have spi-1  mutations in them  and spi-1 is considered therefore a  cancer predisposition gene by many  without a playbook dr romberg offered  the family two options  luke could continue to receive antibody  replacement therapy  and live with the predisposition to  cancer or undergo  a risky bone marrow transplant to reduce  the chance of cancer  and possibly jump start his immunity  what did they tell you were the risks  of a bone marrow transplant to luke that  was a hard conversation i think  it was maybe 10 chance of organ failure  10  mortality rate was a basically it was a  ten to one  odds there were also complications that  maybe would make the quality of his life  worse we really  wrestled with the decision we were  really back and forth i remember  in 2018 when luke was just under two  years old  they made the difficult decision to go  ahead with the transplant  jack their older son was his donor  neither mom michelle  or dad robert were a match  after the transplant and seven weeks in  the hospital  luke's family says his condition  improved  [Music]  they also say it created a lifelong bond  between the brothers  who now share identical dna  who's your best friend jack  jack who's your best friend luki  but the hope that the transplant would  help him produce his own antibodies  which usually occurs in the first year  has not yet worked  one two three for the rest of his life  he may need bi-monthly antibody  infusions and after  three years luke's disease finally has a  name  it is pu1 mutated gamma globulinemia  i've  had some practice saying that word in  the past few years hey guys  while luke's future is uncertain his  doctors believe he will be able to lead  a full life  and he thanks his hero dr romberg  for patients who have diseases who have  not yet been discovered  i think this story is emblematic of how  science can lead the way  we have the capability to really drill  down  on the biology of genetic diseases but  it really does take a partnership  between patients their families doctors  and  scientists since luke's diagnosis they  have found 10 others with this disorder  around the world  and they are now working on a therapy  which gives patients like luke  a new lease on life what are your hopes  for luke for the future  just to be able to lead a full normal  life like any any other kid  and i think that he's even though this  isn't completely  solved he's most of the way there i want  luke  to live cautiously in the ways he needs  to but not  in fear i want him to be adventurous and  daring  and old and not afraid of things so  we're really excited  to let him experience life more  the family says that almost all of  luke's treatments were covered by their  insurance  the work at the hospital was funded by  the jeffrey modell foundation  named after jeffrey modell a young boy  who was born with  and died from a mysterious immune  deficiency his parents established the  non-profit hoping his legacy  will help other children through early  diagnosis treatments and cures  through medical research it's so hard to  see that little body with all the wires  coming out of it and then to know the  parents went to the doctor and there's a  there's no diagnosis so you turn to your  doctor and you say okay how do we fix it  and they say we're not sure  you know my five-year-old daughter saw  this story last night it showed a turn  she said mommy why would god give a  little boy  a disorder like this and you know we  have no answers obviously but  jeffrey modell's family they have a  website where they talk about his story  and they say that jeffrey taught them so  much they taught them that to succeed in  life  you need a wishbone a backbone and a  funny bone  i love that you know and these children  the legacy that they leave  and that is really what they teach us  through dealing with these types  of disorders and their families i think  it's interesting the doctor says it has  to be a partnership  it's not just a partnership these  parents took a big risk they did  absolutely it was there was no playbook  that's what dr what i find remarkable is  that they didn't  they hadn't recognized it in humans  before this and now they've found 10  other people  that have similar conditions that's the  science is remarkable it's amazing and  how many more others right i mean it's  such a huge step  yeah i was going to say that the  community of rare disease  havers is actually quite large in this  country and they're all so isolated the  only thing they have in common is  no one knows what they have that's right  and that's so hard in medicine when you  don't have a diagnosis that's such a  difficult thing to tell a patient  so you know beautiful story beautiful  family things are moving in the right  direction  that's good news thank you tara

